 to  even begin becoming ready  and they are very very nervous well  what's your read of it though i mean i  suppose you i mean you could say it's  just poor organization  uh or you could say that perhaps it's  it's the mark of a government which  doesn't  which won't let it happen  i don't think the government has any  capacity to stop it  at the moment the transition the  opportunity to extend the transition  period ended five months ago  maybe if the government had turned  around and decided they needed an  extension  three four months ago and turned around  to the european union and said hey  we missed that deadline but we changed  our minds can we do something  maybe the european union would have  extended it but  at this point i don't think there's  capacity to extend transition  the negotiations over what that would  look like would probably be more complex  than what's left on the table in the fta  um in the free trade agreement  negotiations so i don't think they have  the capacity to extend it  whether this is a function of government  disorganization  i'm not in a position to say what i will  say is it feels like  active preparations began far later in  the uk  than they did in the european union  businesses had information far later  construction began far later and i think  that was a function in part of politics  while i think the brexit project had a  specter of doubt over it of whether it  would be overturned or  whether there'll be a second referendum  or whatever um the government didn't  feel like it had the freedom  to admit there would be any negative  consequences from brexit at the end of  the transition  well i suppose i only that they felt  like they could  we're running out of time i suppose we  are where we are um  dimitri  i'm just really wondering whether i mean  we talk  a lot don't we about the three sticking  points here which have been the same  sticking points for a long time you've  got competition you've got governance  and you've got fish how stuck are those  and  what's your perspective on which of  those might give  from a trade perspective there are  landing zones on all three  where both sides would get economically  what they need  on how politically stuck they are how  much boris johnson feels like he could  sell a  compromise a halfway on fish or  level playing field or state aid to his  party  and how much the michelle barnier and  the commission  feel like they could sell a compromise  to the eu member states  that's a much much more difficult  question and one i don't think anyone  really knows the answer to  we'll just have to wait and see yes  indeed we will won't we uh dimitri  uh groszabinski thank you very much for  your time on sky news thank you so much
 

